  "question": {
    "text": "<p>The titration curve of weak acid vs. strong base with phenolphthalein as indicator&nbsp;is shown below. The&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K</mi><mtext>phenolphthalein&#160;</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>&#215;</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow></msup></math></p><p>Given: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>log</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>=</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>3</mn></math></p><p><img src=\"https://cdn-question-pool.getmarks.app/pyq/jee_main/12ef0859-3b37-4d9c-9427-f3c6df4364b6.png\" style=\"width: 300px; height: 246px;\" /></p><p>The number of following statement/s which is/are correct about phenolphthalein is _______</p><p>A. It can be used as an indicator for the titration of weak acid with weak base.</p><p>B. It begins to change colour at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>pH</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>=</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mn>8</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>4</mn></math></p><p>C. It is a weak organic base</p><p>D. It is colourless in acidic medium</p><p>&nbsp;</p>",
    "image": null
  },
  "options": [],
  "correctValue": "2",
  "solution": {
    "text": "<blockquote><p>Phenolphthalein is an organic acid and can be represented as <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>HPh</mi></math>.</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><munder><mi>HPh</mi><mtext>&#160;(colourless)&#160;</mtext></munder><mo>&#8652;</mo><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo>+</mo><munder><msup><mi>Ph</mi><mo>-</mo></msup><mtext>&#160;(Pink)&#160;</mtext></munder></math></p><p>Using Henderson equation for phenolphthalein</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>pH</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>pK</mi><mi>In</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mi>log</mi><mfenced close=\"]\" open=\"[\"><mfrac><mfenced close=\"]\" open=\"[\"><msup><mi>Ph</mi><mo>-</mo></msup></mfenced><mrow><mo>[</mo><mi>HPh</mi><mo>]</mo></mrow></mfrac></mfenced></math></p><p>At equivalence point&nbsp;&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfenced close=\"]\" open=\"[\"><msup><mi>Ph</mi><mo>-</mo></msup></mfenced><mo>=</mo><mo>[</mo><mi>HPh</mi><mo>]</mo></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>&#8756;</mo><msub><mi>pH</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>pK</mi><mi>In</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mo>-</mo><mi>log</mi><mo>[</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>]</mo><mo>+</mo><mn>10</mn><mo>=</mo><mo>-</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>6</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>10</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>9</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>4</mn></math></p><p>Hence at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>(</mo><mn>9</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>&#177;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>)</mo><mi>PH</mi></math>&nbsp;, phenolphthalein starts changing colour. Phenolphthalein is colourless in acidic medium and pink in basic medium. Phenolphthalein indicator distinguish the pH change between&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>8</mn></math>&nbsp;to&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>10</mn></math>. Therefore, it is used for strong acid and strong base titration or weak acid and strong base titration. Hence, A and C are incorrect statements.</p></blockquote>",
    "image": null
  },
